population:
  name: "Principales resultados por AGEB y manzana urbana 2020 (INEGI, 2026)"
  data_dir: MX/INEGI/mexicali_poblacion_derivada_2020.geojson
  data_type: vector
  resolution: AGEB
  vector_population_data_field: POBTOT
  population_denominator: POBTOT
  pop_min_threshold: 1
  crs_name: Mexico ITRF2008 / LCC
  crs_standard: EPSG
  crs_srid: 6372
  source_url: https://www.inegi.org.mx/contenidos/programas/ccpv/iter/zip/resageburb20/resageburb_02csv20.zip
  year_published: 2026
  year_target: 2020
  date_acquired: 20260416
  licence: CC BY 4.0
  citation:  "INEGI (2026). Principales resultados por AGEB y manzana urbana 2020. https://www.inegi.org.mx/contenidos/programas/ccpv/iter/zip/resageburb20/resageburb_02csv20.zip."
  notes:  | 
    Spatial data containing AGEB population estimates were derived using official INEGI data.  CSV population estimates were joined to 02a.shp restricted to Mexicali using a query ("CVE_LOC"='0001' AND "CVE_MUN"='002') using the AGEB identified.  The linked spatial data for Mexicali were then exported as a geojson.  The AGEB geometries are contained in the same spatial download as used for the study region boundary (https://en.www.inegi.org.mx/contenidos/productos/prod_serv/contenidos/espanol/bvinegi/productos/geografia/marcogeo/794551163061/02_bajacalifornia.zip).  The spatial data are stored as "conjunto_de_datos/02a.shp".
    BUT
    The population data are finer grained -- for manzanas (blocks; n=13,611); better would be to find the finer grained spatial data representing these.  For now, I will aggregate the blocks to AGEB (n=436).
    
    ... maybe use virtual table in qgis? 
    
